
{"id":"7da1f37b-9f63-49b5-998b-1b1e1bba7b49","object":"chat.completion","created":1784986042,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns {removed_node_ids: [...], retained_node_ids: [...]}. The task is grammar-guided node reduction on a tree, given nodes with ids, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree of nodes. We want to remove some removable nodes while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the retained tree, and also that we cannot remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, all its descendants become removed too? Actually typical tree reduction: if you remove a node, you replace it with its children? But here \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" The problem says: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have grammar info other than symbols. The \"grammar_valid\" likely means that after removal, the tree must still be valid according to a grammar. But we don't have grammar rules. However the task says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nWe need to decide which nodes to remove. The goal is to remove removable nodes to reduce the tree while preserving required symbols. The \"interestingness oracle\" might be represented by the \"utility\" values? Possibly we want to maximize utility? But not specified. The output just requires listing removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. It seems we need to simulate a reduction algorithm.\n\nGiven the description, it appears to be a simplification algorithm: start from the root, consider nodes in order (deeper first, then by ID tie break). For each removable node, check if removing it would still keep all required symbols present somewhere in the retained tree (i.e., among retained nodes). Also cannot remove if it has retained children (meaning children that are not removed? Actually if we remove a node, its children would become children of its parent? That's typical replacement. But the constraint \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (not removed). Because if we remove the node, children would be orphaned? Possibly the tree is such that removing a node means deleting the whole subtree? Let's read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" If a node has children that are retained, you can't remove it because those children would be lost. So removal must be of leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree is being removed. But also \"replacement candidates\" - maybe you can replace a node with one of its children? But not clear.\n\nGiven the input format and standard tree reduction (like Perses), the idea is: attempt to remove each node (and its subtree) if it is removable, and if doing so still satisfies required symbols and some grammar validity. But we have no grammar rules. However the \"required_symbols\" list is provided. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains in the retained set after all removals.\n\nThe algorithm likely: start with all nodes retained. Then iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending (deeper first), and for each node that is removable, check if removing it (i.e., marking it and its subtree as removed) still leaves all required symbols present somewhere in the tree (including possibly other nodes with that symbol). Also check that the node does not have any retained children? That might be circular. Perhaps the condition is: node can be removed only if none of its children are retained (i.e., its entire subtree is already removed or being removed). But since we process deeper first, children will be processed before parents, so when we consider a node, its children may have already been removed or not. If a child is retained, we cannot remove the parent because that would remove the child. So we should only remove a node if all its children are already removed (or not present). But also need to ensure that after removal, required symbols are still present in the entire tree (including other branches). Since we are removing only the subtree rooted at this node, we need to check if any required symbol would be lost that is only present in that subtree. So we need to know the set of symbols present in the subtree of each node.\n\nThus algorithm: For each node, compute the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Also compute the total set of symbols in the whole tree. Then process nodes in order: depth descending, then ID ascending (tie break). For each node that is removable and not root, if removing its subtree (i.e., all nodes in its subtree) would still leave all required symbols present in the remaining tree, then remove it. But also if the node has children that are retained? Actually if we remove a node, we remove all its descendants regardless. So we don't care if children are retained; they would be removed. However the constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" suggests we must not remove a node if any child is retained (meaning we are not removing that child). But in our process, if we decide to remove the node, we will also remove all children, so they become removed, not retained. So maybe the constraint is that we cannot remove a node if there exists a child that we have decided to keep (i.e., not remove). Since we process deeper first, when we consider a parent, we have already decided about children. If any child is marked as retained, then we cannot remove the parent. That makes sense: we only remove a node if the entire subtree is being removed (i.e., all nodes in it are removable and we choose to remove them). So algorithm: process nodes in reverse depth order. For each node, if it is removable, and if all its descendants are either already removed or not present (but since we process children first, they would have been processed earlier), then we can consider removing it. But we also need to check required symbols. So we need to simulate the removal: maintain a set of nodes that are currently removed. Initially none. For each node (deepest first), if it is removable, we check if removing it (i.e., adding it and all nodes in its subtree to removed set) would still leave all required symbols present in nodes not removed. But careful: if we remove a node, we also remove its descendants. Overlap: descendants might already be removed, that's fine. So we need to compute the current \"available\" symbols from nodes not yet removed. Then for each candidate node, we check if after removing its subtree (which may partially already be removed), the remaining available symbols still cover required_symbols. If yes, we mark the node and its entire subtree as removed (add to removed set). Else, we keep it and its subtree (retained). This is a greedy algorithm.\n\nWe also need to handle tie-breaking: if two nodes at same depth, order by ID.\n\nAdditionally, the constraint \"Never remove the root\" - root is not removable (removable: false). Also \"never remove a required symbol\" - but nodes with required symbol might be removable? The problem says \"never remove the root, a required symbol\". So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed, regardless of removable flag? Actually the node's symbol field is string, and required_symbols list includes symbols. So we should treat any node whose symbol is in required_symbols as not removable (even if removable=true). Similarly, \"never remove a node with retained children\" we already incorporated by checking if children are retained (i.e., not removed). But in our greedy approach, if a child is retained (not removed), the parent cannot be removed because that would remove the child. So we need to check that for a node to be removed, all its children must be already removed (or not existing). Since we process deeper first, children are processed before parent. If any child is retained (i.e., we did not remove it), then we cannot remove the parent. But we also need to ensure that if a child is not removable or is a required symbol, it will be retained, so parent cannot be removed. That matches.\n\nWhat about nodes that are not removable? They stay retained.\n\nSo algorithm steps:\n\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list. Build a mapping from id to node dict. Also build parent-child relationships. Ensure root node (parent null) exists. Also check that all required symbols are present somewhere? Possibly not, but we assume valid.\n\n2. Build subtree symbol sets: for each node, recursively compute set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Also compute total tree symbol set.\n\n3. Initialize removed_ids set. Initialize retention status: we will decide after processing.\n\n4. Sort nodes in descending depth. For same depth, sort by id (string) ascending. (Note: the IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"root\". Use natural string comparison.)\n\n5. Process each node in that order:\n\n   - Skip if node is root? root is depth 0, removable false, but also root is required symbol? root is always present. But root is not removable anyway.\n\n   - Skip if node's symbol is in required_symbols: because we never remove a required symbol. (Even if removable true.)\n\n   - Skip if node is already removed? Actually we are processing each node; but removal might affect descendants only. The node itself may not be removed yet. We will only consider removal if it hasn't been removed already. But since we process in order and removal marks subtree, for nodes that are already removed we skip.\n\n   - Check if node is removable (removable == true). If not, skip.\n\n   - Check if all children of this node are already removed (i.e., in removed_ids). If any child is not removed, then we cannot remove this node because that child would be lost. But is it possible that child is not removable but not removed? Then child would be retained, so we cannot remove parent. So we skip if any child is present in original tree and not in removed_ids. (Children are defined by parent field: we need a list of children for each node.)\n\n   - Simulate removal: compute set of symbol loss if we remove this node's subtree. But since we have already removed some descendants, the subtree actually consists of this node and all descendants that are not already removed? Actually if we have already removed some descendants deeper, then those nodes are already not in retained set. The remaining subtree is the node and its descendants that are still retained. So we need to compute the set of symbols that would be lost if we remove this node and all its currently retained descendants. Because already removed descendants are already gone. So we need to compute the symbols contributed by this node and its retained descendants. We can compute by traversing the subtree (excluding already removed nodes) and collecting symbols. Alternatively, we can maintain a \"retained\" set initially all nodes, then as we remove nodes we remove them from retained. For each decision, we check if after removal (i.e., removing this node and all its descendants that are still retained), the required symbols are still present in the retained set minus the removed. That is, we compute the set of symbols that would be removed: symbols from node and its retained descendants. Then check if (total symbols currently retained - removed_symbols) still contains all required symbols. But careful: total symbols currently retained includes symbols from nodes not yet processed and from nodes that are retained deeper but whose ancestors might be removed later. However, since we process deeper first, when we consider a node, all its descendants have already been decided. So the retained set of descendants is fixed. So we can compute the symbols in the subtree of this node that are still retained (i.e., the node itself if retained, and any descendants that are retained). But note: if a descendant is already removed, it's not in retained set. So we need to compute, for each node, the set of symbols in its \"retained subtree\" at the point of processing. We can precompute total symbols in subtree, and then as we remove nodes, we update. Simpler: process depth-first and maintain a running removal set. For each node we process, we know which of its children are still retained. We can compute the set of symbols from retained children (since children processed first, they have already made decisions). Then if we remove this node, we remove its own symbol and the symbols from its retained children (which are the symbols from the retained portions of those subtrees). So we need to keep track, for each node, the set of symbols from its retained subtree (including itself if retained). When we decide to remove a node, we mark it as removed and update its parent's retained subtree symbol set by removing the node's contribution.\n\nThus we can do a bottom-up traversal: For each node, initially, its retained_symbols = {self.symbol} plus the union of retained_symbols of its children (since children not yet processed? Actually we process depth-first, so we need to process children before parent. So we can process in descending depth order, which is bottom-up. For each node, we already know the final retention status of its children (since those at higher depth are processed earlier). So we can compute node's retained_symbols as: if node is removable and we decide to remove it, then its retained_symbols becomes empty; else, it is {self.symbol} union the retained_symbols of each child (but if child was removed, its retained_symbols is empty). So we can store retained_symbols per node after decision.\n\nAlgorithm refined:\n\n- Build tree: nodes dict by id, also list of children for each node.\n\n- Initialize a dict retained_symbols for each node = None.\n\n- Sort nodes by descending depth, then id ascending.\n\n- For each node in sorted order:\n\n    - Compute children_retained_symbols_union = union of retained_symbols[child] for each child (if child id in children list). Initially for leaf nodes, no children, so union empty.\n\n    - If node is not removable, or node's symbol in required_symbols, or node is root? Then we must retain it. Set retained_symbols[node] = {node.symbol} union children_retained_symbols_union. And we mark node as retained (i.e., not removed).\n\n    - Else, we consider removal: we can remove if removing this node still allows all required symbols to be present in the tree overall. How to check? We need to know the global set of symbols that will be retained after all decisions. But we are making decisions greedily and locally. We can maintain a global dictionary of remaining symbol counts or presence. Simpler: we can precompute the total symbols in the whole tree (all nodes). Then, when considering removal, we need to check if the required symbols would still be present in the tree after removing this node and its descendants (including any already removed? But since we process deeper first, all descendants have been processed, so we know which descendants are retained or not. The removal of this node would remove its own symbol and the symbols from its retained children subtrees (since those children are retained only if they were not removed; if they were removed, their symbols are already gone). So the set of symbols that would be additionally removed is: node.symbol (if not already removed? node itself is currently retained because we haven't removed it yet) plus the union of retained_symbols of its children (which are the symbols from retained children subtrees). But note: the children's retained_symbols already include the children's own symbols and their own descendants. So we can compute the set of symbols that would be removed as: removed_set = {node.symbol} ∪ children_retained_symbols_union. Then we need to check if the global set of symbols that will remain after removal still contains all required_symbols. What is the global set after removal? Initially, the global set of symbols in the whole tree is total_symbols = set of all node symbols. As we process, we are building retained_symbols for each node. The global retained symbols after final decisions will be the union of retained_symbols of the root (since root retains all that is kept). But at the moment of decision, we haven't fully computed root's retained_symbols because we process bottom-up. However, we can compute the current \"total symbols in the tree that are not yet removed and not in the subtree of the node\" + \"symbols that will be retained in other branches\". Since we process bottom-up, the symbols outside the subtree of the node are already fixed? Actually all nodes outside the subtree of the node have not been processed yet because they are at same depth or shallower? Wait: Nodes at same depth or shallower are processed later (since we process descending depth). So when processing a node at depth d, all nodes at depth > d have been processed, and nodes at depth <= d have not been processed. The subtree of this node includes only nodes at depth > d (descendants) and itself. Those descendants have been processed. The rest of the tree (other branches at same depth or shallower) are not yet processed. Therefore we cannot know their final retained_symbols. However, we can make a conservative assumption: we want to ensure that after removal, required symbols are still present. We can compute the set of symbols that will definitely be retained: those from nodes that are already processed and retained, but outside the subtree? Actually processed nodes are all at greater depth, which are descendants of nodes at this depth or higher. But the symbols from outside this subtree are from nodes that are not descendants of this node. Those nodes have not been processed yet, but we know their symbols exist in the tree. They may be removed later. So to be safe (greedy), we should only allow removal if the required symbols are already present in the subtree that will be retained after removal? That seems overly strict. Possibly the intended algorithm is to consider removal only if the required symbols are still represented somewhere in the tree, assuming we do not remove any other nodes. Since we are processing bottom-up, we can assume that nodes outside the current subtree will be processed later and may or may not be removed. But we cannot guarantee they will be retained. So the only safe way is to require that after removing this node's subtree, the required symbols are still present elsewhere, i.e., the total symbols from the rest of the tree (including nodes not yet processed) must cover all required symbols. Since we don't know which of those nodes will be removed, we should consider the worst case: that all other nodes might be removed. But that would make it very hard to remove anything. Alternatively, the problem statement says: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This likely means after the removal, considering the current state of retained nodes (including all nodes that have not been removed yet). Since we are processing in order, we are making tentative removals; the final state is the result of all decisions. The greedy approach often works: if at the time of consideration, removing this node would cause loss of a required symbol that is not present elsewhere (in nodes that are currently retained and not in this subtree), then we don't remove. Because we can only rely on nodes that are currently retained (which includes all nodes not yet decided, because we haven't removed them yet). For nodes not yet processed (outside subtree), they are currently all retained (since we haven't decided to remove them). So we can treat them as retained for this check. That is, the current retained set is all nodes minus those that have been removed so far (which are only deeper nodes that we removed). Since we process deeper first, the only removed nodes are descendants of some nodes. So the retained set includes all nodes except those in removed subtrees. So when considering a node, we can compute the set of symbols that would remain after removing this node's subtree (which includes the node and its retained descendants). The symbols from other parts of the tree (including subtrees of other branches and ancestors) are currently all retained. So we can check if the union of symbols from those other parts plus the symbols from the part of the tree that would be retained after removal (i.e., everything except this subtree) contains all required symbols. Since we have not yet processed those other parts, we cannot know if they will later be removed. But we are making a greedy decision: we assume they will stay (since removal decisions are independent). This is typical for such reduction algorithms. And the problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" It doesn't mention any further constraints like maximizing utility. So likely the expected solution is exactly this greedy bottom-up approach.\n\nTherefore, we maintain a global set of symbols that are currently present in the tree (excluding already removed nodes). Initially, the global symbol set is total_symbols. As we remove a node's subtree, we remove its symbols from the global set. For each removable node, we check if the global set would still contain all required symbols if we removed this node's subtree. If yes, we remove it: mark node as removed, update global_symbols by subtracting the symbols from its subtree (using the retained_symbols of its children that are still currently retained). And we also set retained_symbols[node] = empty set (or we can just set to empty). If not, we retain it: we combine its symbol and children's retained_symbols into its own retained_symbols, and keep global_symbols unchanged (no removal). This works because when we process a node, its children's retained_symbols are already finalized (since processed). So we can compute the symbols that would be removed by removing this node: node.symbol (if node not already removed) plus the retained_symbols of its children (which are the symbols from the retained parts of subtrees). But note: if a child was removed, its retained_symbols is empty. So we only count symbols from retained children. That matches: the subtree of the node includes the node and all its descendants that are still retained. When we remove the node, we remove all of that. So the additional loss is exactly that set.\n\nWe must also handle root: root is never removed. For root, we just compute retained_symbols = {root.symbol} ∪ children_retained_symbols_union. That will give the final overall retained symbols.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Build adjacency: children list for each node from parent field.\n\n- Build mapping of node to its depth (already given in input) and its symbol, removable.\n\n- Build dictionary required_symbols set.\n\n- Precompute total_symbols = set of all node symbols.\n\n- Initialize global_symbols = total_symbols (copy).\n\n- Initialize removed_ids = set().\n\n- Initialize retained_symbols dict for each node to set() (will be filled).\n\n- Sort nodes list by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\n- For each node in sorted order:\n    - If node id in removed_ids: continue (maybe not needed as we process once, but children removed? Actually we might have already removed a node if its parent was removed? No, we process bottom-up, so when we remove a parent later, its children are already processed. But if we remove a node, we don't process it again. So we skip if node already removed? Actually we will not process removed nodes because they won't be in the list? They are in the list, but we can check if node id in removed_ids: continue. However, we only remove nodes during the iteration, but we are iterating over a copy of nodes list. The order is fixed; we won't revisit. So we can just proceed, but need to ensure we don't recompute children for removed node. It's simpler to skip if already removed.\n\n    - Compute children_union = set(). For each child_id in children[node.id]:\n        - If child_id in removed_ids: skip (its retained_symbols already empty, but we can still union empty)\n        - children_union.update(retained_symbols[child_id])  # retained_symbols for child should already be set from previous iteration\n\n    - If node.symbol in required_symbols or node.removable == False or node.parent is None (root):\n        - # Must retain\n        - retained_symbols[node.id] = {node.symbol} | children_union\n        - # global_symbols unchanged (since no removal)\n    - Else:\n        - # Candidate for removal\n        - # Symbol set that would be removed: node.symbol + children_union\n        - loss = {node.symbol} | children_union\n        - # Check if after removal, required symbols still in global_symbols - loss\n        - if required_symbols.issubset(global_symbols - loss):\n            # Remove\n            removed_ids.add(node.id)\n            # Update global_symbols by subtracting loss\n            global_symbols -= loss\n            retained_symbols[node.id] = set()  # no retained symbols\n        else:\n            # Retain\n            retained_symbols[node.id] = {node.symbol} | children_union\n            # global_symbols unchanged\n\nAfter processing all nodes, the retained_ids set is all node ids not in removed_ids. The root and all retained nodes are those.\n\nBut we must ensure that the global_symbols is correctly maintained. Initially global_symbols = total_symbols. After each removal, we subtract the loss. This works.\n\nNote: loss may include symbols that are already removed from global_symbols due to previous removals? Actually loss includes node.symbol and children_union. children_union includes only symbols from retained children (those not removed). So those symbols are still in global_symbols because they haven't been removed yet. node.symbol is also still in global_symbols because node is not yet removed. So all symbols in loss are present in global_symbols at this point. So subtracting is safe.\n\nEdge cases: What if two removable nodes have overlapping subtrees? Because we process bottom-up, when we process a child, we may remove it. Then when we process parent, its retained children won't include that child because child was removed, so children_union will be empty for that child. So parent's loss set is just its own symbol (if none of its children are retained). That's correct.\n\nTie-breaking: For nodes at same depth, we sort by id ascending. IDs are strings; we can use string comparison.\n\nNow, we need to produce output: removed_node_ids list (in removal order) and retained_node_ids list (sorted). The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order should be the order in which nodes were removed. Since we process in descending depth order and only remove when condition met, the removal order is the order of iteration that decided removal. We should collect removed_ids in the order they were added. So we can maintain a list removed_order and append node.id when we decide to remove.\n\nFor retained_node_ids, we need to return sorted list of all node ids not in removed_ids. Sort lexicographically? It doesn't specify sort order for retained, but it says \"sorted retained IDs\". Likely ascending string sort.\n\nAlso need to ensure that root and required symbols are never removed. Our logic already handles that.\n\nNow, confirm with the fixture examples. Let's test with the first case manually (case 000). Nodes: root (depth0, symbol root, removable false), n1 (depth1, s1, removable false), n2 (depth1, s2, removable true), n3 (depth1, s0, removable false), n4 (depth2, s1, removable true, parent n2), n5 (depth2, s2, removable false, parent n3), n6 (depth2, s0, removable true, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nWe process depth first: deepest depth=2: n4, n5, n6. Depth=1: n1, n2, n3. Depth=0: root.\n\nInitialize global_symbols = {root, s1, s2, s0} (from root, n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2), n6(s0) -> set = {root, s1, s2, s0}.\n\nProcess depth2 nodes sorted by id: n4, n5, n6.\n\nn4: symbol s1, removable true, not required (s1 is required? required_symbols includes \"s1\". So n4.symbol is in required_symbols => must retain. So we retain. retained_symbols[n4] = {s1} (no children). global_symbols unchanged (still all). removed_order unchanged.\n\nn5: symbol s2, removable false => retain. retained_symbols[n5] = {s2}.\n\nn6: symbol s0, removable true, not required (required are root,s1). Check children_union: no children. loss = {s0}. Check if required_symbols subset of global_symbols - loss: required = {root,s1}; global minus loss = {root, s1, s2} (since s0 removed). {root,s1} is subset. So we can remove n6. So removed_ids = {'n6'}, global_symbols = {root,s1,s2}, retained_symbols[n6]=set().\n\nNow depth=1 nodes: n1, n2, n3 sorted by id: n1,n2,n3.\n\nn1: symbol s1, removable false, but s1 is required so retain. Children: n6 is a child? n6 parent is n1. n6 is in removed_ids, so skip. children_union empty. retained_symbols[n1] = {s1}.\n\nn2: symbol s2, removable true, not required. Children: n4 (parent n2). n4 retained? n4 was retained (since required symbol). So children_union = retained_symbols[n4] = {s1}. loss = {s2} ∪ {s1} = {s2, s1}. Check required subset of global_symbols - loss: global_symbols = {root, s1, s2}; loss = {s1,s2}; global minus loss = {root}. required = {root,s1}. s1 not present, so not subset. Thus we cannot remove n2. So retain n2: retained_symbols[n2] = {s2} ∪ {s1} = {s1,s2}.\n\nn3: symbol s0, removable false => retain. Children: n5 (parent n3). n5 retained? n5 was retained (removable false). children_union = retained_symbols[n5] = {s2}. retained_symbols[n3] = {s0} ∪ {s2} = {s0,s2}.\n\nNow depth0 root: symbol root, removable false => retain. Children: n1,n2,n3. children_union = retained_symbols[n1] ∪ retained_symbols[n2] ∪ retained_symbols[n3] = {s1} ∪ {s1,s2} ∪ {s0,s2} = {s0,s1,s2}. retained_symbols[root] = {root} ∪ children_union = {root, s0, s1, s2}. Final removed_ids = ['n6']. Retained ids: all except n6: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? But typically root is first? Sorted lexicographically: 'n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'. Is that expected? Probably yes.\n\nBut check the expected output? Not given. This seems plausible.\n\nNow test second fixture case 001 similar but different utilities. The algorithm doesn't use utility, only removable flag and required symbols. So the output will depend only on removable flags and required symbols. Let's see: case 001: nodes same structure, n4 removable true, n6 removable true. Same required symbols. n4 symbol s1 is required? Yes, s1 is required. So n4 cannot be removed. n6 symbol s0 not required, so may be removed. That would be same as case 000. So removed n6 only. Case 002 similarly. So output would be identical across these fixtures? But maybe that's correct.\n\nBut the task mentions \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". Here \"interestingness\" might be tied to utility? But we ignored utility. Possibly the algorithm should also consider that when multiple removable nodes are candidates, we might choose to remove the one with lower utility? However the problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't say anything about maximizing utility. The utility values might be used elsewhere or for future tasks. But given the instructions, it seems the core algorithm is deterministic based on tree structure, removable flags, and required symbols.\n\nHowever, the description also says: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We did that. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Done. \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We handled root and required symbol directly. For \"node with retained children\", we implicitly handle because if a child is retained, the parent's loss set includes the child's symbol(s), which may cause required symbol check to fail, but also the condition \"node with retained children\" might be an independent constraint: even if required symbols are still present elsewhere, we cannot remove a node that has any retained child. Our algorithm does not explicitly check that; it relies on the required symbol check. But what if a node has retained children but the required symbols are still satisfied? The constraint says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests it's a hard rule regardless of required symbols. So we must add an explicit check: if the node has any child that will be retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove it. In our greedy process, at the time we consider the node, children have already been processed. If any child is retained (i.e., not in removed_ids), then we must retain the node. So we need to add that condition: if any child of this node is not in removed_ids, then we cannot remove. This matches typical tree reduction where you only remove nodes that are \"dead\" (all children removed). So we need to check: children_all_removed = all(child_id in removed_ids for child_id in children[node.id]) or children list empty. If not all children are removed, then we must retain. This condition should be checked before the required symbol check, and it overrides.\n\nLet's adjust algorithm: For each removable node:\n\n    - Compute children list. If any child not in removed_ids (i.e., retained), then cannot remove -> retain.\n\n    - Else (all children removed or no children), then compute loss and check required symbols.\n\nAdd that.\n\nIn case 000: n6 has no children, so no issue. n2 has child n4 which is retained (n4 not removed) -> so n2 cannot be removed because it has a retained child. That matches our required symbol check also failed, but now we have an explicit stop. Good.\n\nThus we incorporate.\n\nNow we need to produce the Python code. We'll implement solve(case) that takes case (the payload dict). It should return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe must handle possible malformed cases? Raise ValueError if infeasible. Infeasible could be if required_symbols not all present in the tree initially. We should check that. Also if root is not present or other structural issues. We'll validate.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Extract nodes list and required_symbols list from case.\n\n- Build dict node_map = {node['id']: node}.\n\n- Build children dict: for each node, children[node['id']] = [].\n\n- For each node, if node['parent'] is not None: children[node['parent']].append(node['id']).\n\n- Ensure there is exactly one node with parent None? We'll assume.\n\n- Collect all symbols = set(node['symbol'] for node in node_map.values()).\n\n- Required symbols set = set(required_symbols).\n\n- Check if required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols): if not, raise ValueError.\n\n- Sort node ids by depth (descending) and then id (ascending). Use sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda id: (-node_map[id]['depth'], id)).\n\n- Initialize global_symbols = all_symbols.copy().\n\n- removed_ids = set()\n\n- removed_order = [] (list)\n\n- retained_symbols = {} (dict id -> set)\n\n- For each node_id in sorted_ids:\n    node = node_map[node_id]\n    if node_id in removed_ids: continue  # shouldn't happen but safe\n\n    # compute children_union and check children removal\n    child_ids = children.get(node_id, [])\n    all_children_removed = all(cid in removed_ids for cid in child_ids)\n    children_union = set()\n    for cid in child_ids:\n        if cid not in removed_ids:\n            children_union.update(retained_symbols.get(cid, set()))  # retained if present\n    # Actually children_union is only for retained children, but if all children removed, children_union empty\n\n    # determine if must retain\n    must_retain = False\n    if node['parent'] is None:  # root\n        must_retain = True\n    elif node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n        must_retain = True\n    elif not node['removable']:\n        must_retain = True\n    elif not all_children_removed:\n        must_retain = True\n\n    if must_retain:\n        # retain\n        retained_symbols[node_id] = {node['symbol']} | children_union\n        # global_symbols unchanged\n    else:\n        # candidate removal\n        loss = {node['symbol']} | children_union\n        # Check required symbols\n        if required_symbols.issubset(global_symbols - loss):\n            # remove\n            removed_ids.add(node_id)\n            removed_order.append(node_id)\n            global_symbols -= loss\n            retained_symbols[node_id] = set()\n        else:\n            # retain\n            retained_symbols[node_id] = {node['symbol']} | children_union\n\nAfter loop, retained_ids = sorted(set(node_map.keys()) - removed_ids) (string sort). removed_node_ids = removed_order (order of removal).\n\nOutput dict: {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nReturn that.\n\nEdge: If root is"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":8192,"total_tokens":10364,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":8192},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d